# Mastrify: the look and the speed are locked

The site in `dist-v2/` is a finished, approved design. The job in this
project is to connect it to the real engine and Stripe. That job is done by
feeding data in, never by changing how anything looks, moves or loads.
Read `BACKEND-CONTRACT.md` ("The rule" and section 0) before any change.

`BACKEND-CONTRACT.md` is the only spec for this work. If the folder also
has `README.md`, `BACKEND_HANDOFF.md`, `HANDOFF-CLAUDE.md` or `dist/`, ignore
them (the older prototype and the design team's log) and do not touch
`dist/`.

## Files you may edit

- `dist-v2/backend.js`: the adapter. All backend code goes here.
- `dist-v2/config.js`: setting values only.
- `dist-v2/studio-copy.js`: wording only, about the same length, so nothing reflows.

Everything else in `dist-v2/` is locked. Do not edit, reformat, rename,
move, delete or add files there. That includes every `.css`, every other
`.js`, every `.html` page shell and everything in `assets/`.
`mastrify.css`, `mastrify-base.js` and `mastrify-app.js` are generated by
`npm run build`; never edit them.

## Never

- Change colours, fonts, sizes, spacing, layout, borders, shadows, glows,
  animations, timings, easing or transitions. Not even "just a little".
- Add, remove or reorder elements, or add `<script>`, `<link>` or `<style>`
  tags to the pages.
- Add libraries, SDKs, frameworks, fonts, icon sets, analytics, tag
  managers, cookie banners or chat widgets to the site.
- Port the pages into React, Next.js, Vue or any framework, or wrap them in
  a framework layout. Serve the files as they are.
- Refactor, "clean up", modernise, reformat or run Prettier/ESLint --fix on
  the sources. Change the build (`build-v2.mjs`) or `package.json` scripts.
- Run `node build-v2.mjs --lock`. Only the design owner (Linus) approves
  design changes.

If something in the look seems wrong or missing, do not fix it: stop and
tell Linus, with a screenshot.

## Speed rules for backend.js

- At load, only define `window.MastrifyBackend`. No network requests, timers
  or heavy work at load.
- Plain `fetch`. No SDKs in the browser (Stripe Checkout is a redirect; it
  needs no Stripe.js). The build fails the check above 40 KB.
- Call `onProgress` right away, then as often as you can, with `eta` when
  known. Poll the engine at most every 500 to 1000 ms, or use SSE/WebSocket.
- Stop all work (polling, uploads, sockets) when `signal` aborts or the job
  ends.
- Heavy work happens on the server. Do not decode or process audio in the
  browser; the browser already measures the file.

## Before you say you are done

`npm run build`, then `npm run build:check` must print
"design unchanged" and "bundles and pages are up to date". If it prints
DESIGN LOCK, undo the listed changes.
